\begin_layout Section*
Parallelism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A major criticsim of experiments is that results will not hold in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

real world
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for a variety of reasons.
 For example:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Participants in the lab think differently than subjects in the real world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
High stakes aren't present in the lab, and they often can make things worse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The results in a labratory are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

artificial
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 compared to the real world.
 However, this critique can be made against any experimental method.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Naturally occuring processes don't often occur in the lab, but this is often
 a claim for more reasearch in general.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The lab results obtained under one set of arbitrary parameters may not hold
 for another set of arbitrary parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An important response to this criticsim is that experiments test theories,
 and theories should hold in the field.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Designing Experiments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Should one undertake a lab experiment or a field experiment? Well, there
 is trade-off of the realism found in the field versus the abstraction of
 the theory when deciding what kind of experiment to pursue.
 Neither of these extremes should be aimed for while you are designing an
 experiment.
 Instead, focus on the question you wish to answer, and then determine how
 you will try to answer it.
 Design experiments with an eye towards data and the analysis techniques
 you wish to employ.
 How will you generate data that will easily be converted into results?
 Remember to use non-parametris statistical tests over parametric tests.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Also, saliency is incredibly important.
 The payoffs subjects receive should be based on the decisions they make.
 Between-subject design may be cleaner than within-subject design.
 Pay subjects with an amount of cash that is higher than their opportunity
 costs.
 Students are good learners with lower opportunity costs.
 Create complete and unbiased instructions that are easy to understand.
 Run pilot experiments to test for any irregularities.
 Do not deceive or lie.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Agents determine messages under environments (utility, technology, endowments)
 and institutional rules determine outcomes.
 You can vary the institution and compare outcomes, or vice versa.
 Identify the differences between economic institutions and environments.
 Experiments can vary either of these aspects and hold the other constant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Additional Suggestions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Instead of a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

latin square,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 try to limit the amount of treatments you are testing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One must take into account the fact that subjects can learn from trial to
 trial.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Know your subject pool.
 You can manipulate your subjects innate idiosyncrasies by using specific
 wording in the project outline your subjects read before the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Widely separate the treatment levels to make the effects and outcomes clear.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If certain nuisance variables pose significant problem, then you can treat
 them as treatment variables.
\end_layout

